+*Preprint · author-review revision · 7 November 2026*
 
 ## Abstract
 
-The public benchmark landscape for AI in drug development is dominated by **single-stage prediction** (property/ADMET, docking and binding affinity, molecule generation, retrosynthesis). Two decisions that gate real programs — **Phase-I dose selection** and **pharmacokinetic exposure prediction** — have essentially no open, graded agent benchmark, and no existing benchmark grades whether a system carries a decision *correctly across consecutive development stages*. We introduce **DrugDev-Bench**, whose measured version-1 contribution is a pair of execution-relevant decision tasks graded against a mechanistic reference, and whose design includes a cross-stage, execution-grounded track reported here as a roadmap with a pilot, not as a measured headline. We report baselines unedited. On Phase-I dose selection, language models are weak (frontier 0.11–0.50); the **oracle reference — the standard selection rule applied to the hidden true toxicity curve — scores 0.85**, showing that the task is far from solved by any language model. The oracle's three misses of 20 reflect differences between the selection policy and the benchmark's author-defined answers (tie-handling and no-safe-dose criteria), which we discuss as a target-definition question rather than task difficulty. On pharmacokinetic prediction, once the target endpoint is specified unambiguously, capable models reach only ~0.40–0.47 within two-fold. We make no claim of a model capability win; the contribution is an open, mechanistically-anchored decision benchmark on development stages the field has left unmeasured — scored **end-to-end and multi-dimensionally** (the executed outcome alongside safety and exposure–response fidelity), so a wrong answer is localized to *where* the reasoning chain broke.
+Evaluating language models for drug-development decisions requires explicit decision policies and well-defined reference outcomes. Here we present DrugDev-Bench, a methodological benchmark comprising dose selection and pharmacokinetic exposure tasks. The available datasets contain 40 dose scenarios and 30 compound cases, divided equally between hand-authored and generated tiers. An oracle selection rule supplied with the hidden toxicity curve agrees with 17 of 20 hand-authored dose labels; its three disagreements expose differences between the rule and reference-label policy. In structured-response experiments, reported hand-tier dose accuracy is 0.25–0.30 for Claude Opus 5 and 0.10 for GPT-6.1-sol. Both models attain 1.00 in a generated-tier run with explicit exposure–response parameters, although an additional Opus run scores 0.95. On 15 hand-authored pharmacokinetic cases, the best recorded within-twofold agreement is 0.467. These results reveal sensitivity to problem specification and reference construction. They support evaluating decision accuracy alongside curve agreement and response completeness, while distinguishing simplified modeled tasks from validated pharmacokinetic prediction or clinical dose-finding performance.
 
